\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two positive steps with different slice data}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Take dimension #{2}, a scalar fiber, normal and transverse
spacings #{1}, mass zero and two layers. Let the normal link be
the identity. On a one-site transverse circle give the shifts
on successive layers the scalar values #{-1} and #{\ui}.
For the first [step](fdip-01BF) the centered term vanishes
and #{B_{\pm,1}=3\bbI_\pm}. Thus #{T_1} acts by #{1/3}
on #{W_+} and #{3} on #{W_-}. For the second step
#{B_{\pm,2}=2\bbI_\pm} and #{E_2=\ui P_+E_j|_{W_-}\ne0}
for the transverse direction #{j}. Both steps are positive by
\ref{fdip-01BI}. The second mixes the two normal spaces
on which the first acts by different scalars. Thus
#{T_2T_1\ne T_1T_2=(T_2T_1)^\dagger}.
On any larger transverse circle the same calculation applies
to the constant character with these layerwise scalar links.}
